# CONTROL for selector-field.go, and it fails in the entertaining direction.
#
# `int(x)` is a genuine type conversion - the one construct
# `type_conversion_expression` exists for. Both parsers call it a
# `call_expression`:
#
#   call_expression [41, 47)
#     function: identifier   "int"
#     arguments: argument_list [44, 47)
#
# tree-sitter-go does this on purpose. Without a selector there is nothing to
# tell `int(x)` from a call of a function named `int`, so the grammar does not
# try, and the conversion rule is reserved for the shapes where a type is
# syntactically forced.
#
# Which pins the guilty witness precisely. Joints does not over-eagerly reach
# for `type_conversion_expression`: on the one input that really is a
# conversion it does not reach for it at all. It reaches for it only when a
# `.` is in play, because that is the only place the rival derivation exists.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call_expression
lacks type_conversion_expression
lacks qualified_type
spans call_expression 41 47
